  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 PM EDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\n- Unsettled pattern over the next 7 days\n\nUnsettled conditions are expected over the course of the next 7\ndays with upper troughing overhead or nearby much of the time. At\nthe surface, mid summer moisture levels will be in place with dew\npoints at least in the 60s if not around 70 degrees. This level of\nmoisture combined with some mid level cooling due to troughing in\nthe mid levels will set the stage for some build up of afternoon \nand evening instability each day. Bottom line is we are looking\nperiodic chances for showers and storms through the period. The\nmain question is trying to nail down the time frames for the best\nchances for showers/storms. \n\nAs for the details, the first good opportunity for showers and\nstorms will occur Wednesday into Wednesday night as a weak cold\nfront moves in from the northwest. Chances actually begin late\ntonight as the front approaches in the northwest CWA. We have\nlikely pops at times in our gridded forecast late tonight through\nWednesday night. Most unstable CAPE values increase into the\n500-1000 j/kg range at times so thunder is certainly possible.\nLocally heavy rain will likely be the main threat. \n\nThe front goes south of the forecast area but remains nearby\nThursday into Friday. Some model differences make the remainder of\nthe forecast a bit lower confidence given a rather benign surface\npattern. Given a predominant northwest flow/upper trough in the\nmid level it is hard to rule out at least small pops Thursday\nthrough next Monday. We could see some rain totals add up in spots\nthrough the 7 days, but rivers have plenty of room given low\nflows. Some short term advisories are certainly possible given the\nmoisture values (approaching 2 inches at times).\n\n- Seasonable temperatures are expected through the forecast period\n\nTemperatures look to remain seasonable despite the upper\ntroughing. 850mb temperatures are in the middle teens C which \nmake highs in the 80s likely each day.",
